Newport framed console: build and regression
============================================

Build sys/arch/sgimips/conf/NEWPORT_FRAME for sgimips/mipseb.  It includes
GENERIC32_IP2x and enables NEWPORT_FRAME, which implies NEWPORT_DECOR.  The
generic kernels continue to use the black-to-green gradient.  Test all three
builds: framed, gradient only, and a local configuration with neither option.

At 1280x1024 or 1282x1024 the text viewport is x=288, y=176, width=704,
height=672.  With an 8x16 font, TIOCGWINSZ must report 42 rows and 88 columns.
Early cnattach and vcons attachment use the same viewport.  At other sizes
the frame option falls back to the full-screen gradient.  Firmware output
before the NetBSD console attaches remains controlled by the PROM.

The asset and regeneration instructions are in
sys/arch/sgimips/gio/newport-decor/README.  All 161 source colours are retained.
The PNG is converted into constant palette/rectangle data ahead of kernel
builds.  The panel is uniform, allowing hardware scrolling before vcons
attaches while the outer image stays fixed.

Run newport_decor.c as described in README.newport-decor.  Capture the native
visible framebuffer, excluding any emulator UI.  Check that:

  - Blank clears reproduce sgiconsole.png exactly (crop the last two columns
    of the reference for a 1280-wide display).
  - Forward/reverse scrolling, column insertion/deletion, and erasure do not
    move or damage the backdrop; cursor hide restores the exact prior pixels.
  - The a command makes only the text rectangle red, including its final
    row and column.  The bevel and padding retain their source pixels.
  - n and N produce identical text and colours after scrolling three rows.
  - Underline, reverse video, and explicit background colours work inside
    the panel.  Long messages wrap within the reported width.
  - m followed by c restores the entire prior text image and backdrop.
  - Creating ttyE1, selecting it, and returning to ttyE0 restores each
    screen's contents, frame, padding, and 42x88 geometry.
  - Boot-message replay, normal shell output, and coloured full clears never
    change pixels outside the text viewport.

For RGB captures, compare every pixel outside [288,992) x [176,848) with the
reference PNG after each operation.  Also compare the entire image for blank
scroll/cursor checks and n/N or text/mode-restoration pairs.  Wait for DONE
and rendering completion before capturing.  VT switching is asynchronous;
wait for the switch to finish before typing c to hide the restored cursor.
Raw framebuffer-index checks
are useful for diagnosing emulators, but do not establish correct palette
or final display composition.

Keep disposable disk/NVRAM copies and record exact emulator revisions or
binary hashes.  A stale composed frame can differ from the raw framebuffer;
inspect both before changing the driver to compensate for an emulator bug.
Physical hardware still needs timing and performance validation.

Validation on 2026-11-02
------------------------

Framed, gradient-only, and option-disabled mipseb kernels built successfully.
On an R4400 Indy with 256 MB RAM and a 1282x1024 Newport display, the guest
reported 42 rows and 88 columns.  The frame matched the source PNG exactly
outside the text rectangle during boot and the console exerciser.

The baseline emulator sources were:

    Iris      a9b63183c130a23b199f82f01000b51cf70f26e2
    Prismatic cd1b0315b339bb859c4cad07bd0a5db2d89ceaf5

Iris checks used raw framebuffer-index captures because this build's composed
offscreen screenshot is black.  Prismatic checks used its native RGB capture.
Blank forward/reverse scrolling, column operations, erasure, cursor movement,
numbered coloured text scrolling, and mode restoration matched their
reference captures.  Pixel comparisons confirmed that all these operations
preserved the image outside the viewport.
Prismatic also reported 42x88 on a newly allocated ttyE1 and restored the
exact ttyE0 text image after a VT round trip, with the cursor hidden again.
Nine captures from early kernel output through the single-user shell prompt
preserved the source image outside the viewport, including the early NetBSD
banner before vcons attachment.

Prismatic display-refresh limitation: a large solid colour clear followed
by no further drawing can leave a partial clear in the composed capture,
even though rex fbdump shows all 704x672 text pixels correctly filled.
The exerciser's a command reproduces it; c triggers another redraw and the
RGB capture then shows the complete red rectangle with the frame intact.
This occurred with the baseline above and with the current test binary
(SHA256 039f55db138ab903717e507655f5ae7dc9c8551ee0cd6dd29a76b7e0d8c96bb9).
The colour-clear check therefore validated the complete raw fill first, then
the RGB result after c.  This is an outstanding emulator display issue, not
an unconditional pass of the idle composed-clear check.
